  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Elevated tide cycles persist due to Post- \nTropical Cyclone Josephine well off the coast, resulting in \nwidespread minor coastal flooding continuing in Ocean County, NJ\nand Sussex County, DE.\n\nAs Post-Tropical Cyclone Josephine lingers well off the coast \nelevated tide cycles are persisting some areas. \n\nIn Ocean County, New Jersey and Sussex County, Delaware, water \nlevels remain elevated and minor tidal flooding is continuing, \nparticularly in the back bays. In fact a few locations in the \nback bays came very close to or even touched moderate levels of \ncoastal flooding yesterday with the high tide. It's going to \ntake some time for water levels to diminish in these areas so \nwe've continued the Coastal Flood Advisory for these counties \nthrough this evening. Elsewhere along the Atlantic coast and \nDelaware Bay any additional coastal flooding looks to be spotty \nat most. \n\nAt this time we are not forecasting any coastal flooding for \nthe Maryland eastern shore. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Frost and freeze impacts likely tonight and \nSaturday night where the growing season continues. \n\nA Freeze Warning was issued for areas north and west of the \nI-95 corridor from midnight until 9AM Saturday where the \ngrowing season remains active. For the I-95 corridor and areas \nsouth and east, a Frost Advisory was issued from midnight until\n9AM Saturday for all other zones, excluding Cape May County in \nNew Jersey, Sussex County in Delaware, and the coastal strip in \nboth states.\n\nAs an upper-level trough pivots across the Northeast tonight, a\nstrong mid-level ridge in the Plains to Midwest shifts eastward\nthrough the weekend. This will support strong surface high \npressure shifting eastward into the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic \nthrough the weekend.\n\nA chilly and dry air mass will accompany this strong surface \nhigh, and as the high itself settles more overhead, forecast\nsoundings show surface winds decoupling. This will set the \nstage for efficient radiational cooling both tonight and \nSaturday night. The forecast lows are in the 30s for much of the\narea (with the only exception along the immediate coastal \nstrip) with a favorable setup for frost and freeze. Some \nguidance does show light upper level stratus developing late \novernight with the best chance for cloud coverage further north \nand east, but confidence remains low. Any light cloud coverage \nwould be the only limiting reagent to the efficient radiational \ncooling and could slow cooling down by a few degrees at best, \nbut it is not expected to slam the brakes hard on the efficient \nradiational cooling setup overnight.\n\nAn additional round of frost/freeze impacts is possible Saturday\nnight through Sunday morning for wherever the growing season\ncontinues.",
